// Demonstration of the `er` package — entity-relationship diagrams in Chen notation, a thin
// layer of conventions over the `diagram` package. This document walks through every shape and
// connector the package provides and ends with a complete database schema that combines them.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/er-demo.script

\use{document}
\use{er}

\title{Entity-Relationship Diagrams}
\author{The \italic{er} package}
\maketitle

// A small helper: each diagram is centred on its own line with a little air above and below.
\def showpic p {\vskip 8pt\centerline{\p}\vskip 8pt}

\section{Chen notation}

In Chen notation an entity-relationship schema is drawn from three kinds of shape, joined by
undirected lines. An \italic{entity} — a thing the database stores facts about — is a
rectangle. A \italic{relationship} — an association between entities — is a diamond. An
\italic{attribute} — a property of an entity or relationship — is an ellipse. The
\italic{cardinality} of each association (1, N, or M) is written beside the line that records
it. The \italic{er} package gives each of these shapes a command of its own, so a diagram reads
almost like its description.

Every command has the same three arguments: a \italic{name} that later connectors refer to, a
\italic{placement} (\verb|at x y|, or \verb|right foo 60|, \verb|above bar|, and so on,
relative to an already-placed shape), and the \italic{label} drawn inside. Because \italic{er}
is a layer over \italic{diagram}, the diagram configuration commands and the raw shape commands
remain available alongside the ones shown here.

\section{Entities}

An ordinary entity is a plain rectangle. A \italic{weak} entity — one whose instances cannot be
identified without the entity it depends on — draws a second outline just inside the first.

\showpic{\picture width:5in height:1in {
  \entity{e1}{at 1in 0.5in}{Employee}
  \weakentity{e2}{right e1 90}{Dependent}
}}

\section{Relationships}

A relationship is a diamond. An \italic{identifying} (weak) relationship — the one that ties a
weak entity to its owner — is drawn as a double diamond.

\showpic{\picture width:5in height:1.1in {
  \relationship{r1}{at 1in 0.55in}{Works For}
  \weakrelationship{r2}{right r1 90}{Has}
}}

\section{Attributes}

Attributes are ellipses, and Chen notation distinguishes four kinds. A plain \italic{attribute}
is a single ellipse. A \italic{key} attribute, part of the entity's primary key, has its label
underlined. A \italic{multivalued} attribute, which may hold several values at once, is a double
ellipse. A \italic{derived} attribute, computed from other data rather than stored, is drawn
with a dashed outline.

\showpic{\picture width:6in height:1in {
  \attribute{a1}{at 0.8in 0.5in}{name}
  \keyattribute{a2}{right a1 35}{ssn}
  \multivalued{a3}{right a2 35}{phones}
  \derived{a4}{right a3 35}{age}
}}

\section{Connectors and cardinalities}

A connector is an undirected line drawn with \verb|\connect|. With no argument it simply joins
two shapes — the usual way to attach an attribute to its entity. Given a bracketed argument it
also writes a cardinality beside the line: \verb|\connect[1]|, \verb|\connect[N]|,
\verb|\connect[M]|. The line is clipped to each shape's boundary, so it meets the rectangle,
diamond, or ellipse cleanly at any angle.

\showpic{\picture width:5in height:1.7in {
  \entity{c1}{at 1in 0.6in}{Course}
  \relationship{c2}{right c1 70}{Taken By}
  \entity{c3}{right c2 70}{Student}
  \attribute{c4}{above c1 30}{title}
  \connect{c1}{c4}
  \connect[M]{c1}{c2}
  \connect[N]{c2}{c3}
}}

A \italic{total participation} constraint — every instance of an entity must take part in the
relationship — is drawn with \verb|\connecttotal|, which doubles the connecting line. It accepts
the same optional cardinality. Here every dependent must belong to some employee, so the line
from the relationship to the weak entity is doubled.

\showpic{\picture width:5in height:1.4in {
  \entity{t1}{at 1in 0.7in}{Employee}
  \weakrelationship{t2}{right t1 70}{Has}
  \weakentity{t3}{right t2 70}{Dependent}
  \connect[1]{t1}{t2}
  \connecttotal[N]{t2}{t3}
}}

\section{A complete schema}

Putting every shape and connector together, the schema below records employees, the departments
they work for, and their dependents. The \italic{Employee} entity carries a key attribute
(\italic{ssn}), an ordinary attribute (\italic{name}), a multivalued attribute (\italic{phones},
since an employee may list several numbers), and a derived attribute (\italic{age}, computed
from a birth date). \italic{Department} has its own key and name. The weak entity
\italic{Dependent} is identified through the double-diamond \italic{Has} relationship, with total
participation: a dependent record cannot exist without its employee.

\showpic{\picture width:6in height:4.6in {
  // Employee attributes, fanned out above the entity.
  \keyattribute{ssn}{at 0.55in 4in}{ssn}
  \attribute{ename}{at 1.55in 4.2in}{name}
  \multivalued{phones}{at 2.7in 4.2in}{phones}
  \derived{age}{at 3.75in 4in}{age}

  // Department attributes, above the department entity.
  \attribute{dname}{at 4.55in 4.2in}{name}
  \keyattribute{dnum}{at 5.55in 4in}{number}

  // The entity — relationship — entity spine.
  \entity{emp}{at 1.3in 2.9in}{Employee}
  \relationship{wf}{at 3.1in 2.9in}{Works For}
  \entity{dept}{at 4.9in 2.9in}{Department}

  // A weak entity, identified by the employee it depends on.
  \weakrelationship{has}{at 1.3in 1.8in}{Has}
  \weakentity{dep}{at 1.3in 0.7in}{Dependent}
  \keyattribute{depname}{at 3in 1.1in}{name}

  // Attribute links.
  \connect{emp}{ssn}
  \connect{emp}{ename}
  \connect{emp}{phones}
  \connect{emp}{age}
  \connect{dept}{dname}
  \connect{dept}{dnum}
  \connect{dep}{depname}

  // Relationship links, with cardinalities.
  \connect[N]{emp}{wf}
  \connect[1]{wf}{dept}
  \connect[1]{emp}{has}
  \connecttotal[N]{has}{dep}
}}
